# Chat architecture

Chat is **account to account**. Characters play no part in it. The previous design keyed messages on
characters and campaigns, which broke every player holding heroes in more than one campaign: the
drawer, the unread badge and the push deep link all followed the *selected* character, so messages to
any other hero were invisible. Do not reintroduce a character or campaign id into the chat path.

Where things live: entities in `DA_DataAccess/Chat`, the access rule in `DA_Business/Services/ChatAccess.cs`,
queries in `DA_Business/Repository/ChatRepos/ChatRepository.cs`, per-circuit UI state in
`DA_Business/Services/ChatState.cs`, push shaping in `GameNotificationDispatcher.PlanChatMessage`.

## Invariants

- **One thread per pair.** `ChatConversation.PairKey` (built only by `ChatPairKey`) is unique, and that
  unique index is the sole guard against duplicate threads — `GetOrCreateAsync` relies on the loser of a
  race failing its insert and re-reading. Never build a pair key by hand.
- **The Game Master is a role, not an account.** A direct thread with the GM holds a `ChatParticipant`
  with `UserId == null` and `IsGameMasterRole`, and its key is `ChatPairKey.Direct(playerId, null)`. When a
  GM writes to a player the thread is still keyed by the *player*, so handing the campaign to another GM
  keeps the history. GMs are excluded from each other's peer lists: a GM is reachable only through the role.
  **Admin counts as GM** in `GameMasterUserIdsAsync` / `NotificationRecipientLookup` — same as `IsAdminOrMG`
  elsewhere — so a seeded Admin-only account still receives chat and barony GM pushes.
- **Read state is per account** (`ChatReadState`), deliberately apart from participation, so one GM
  opening a thread does not clear the badge for the others.
- **Party membership is derived, never stored.** A `CampaignParty` thread has no participant rows; its
  members are the campaign roster plus the GMs, read at query time. Adding a character to a campaign must
  not require touching chat.
- **Read paths never write.** Threads are created on first use (`GetOrCreate*`), so a contact row with
  `ConversationId == null` is normal and means "reachable peer, nothing said yet".
- **Recipients are resolved once,** by `ChatAccess.RecipientsAsync`. The in-process broadcaster and the
  push dispatcher both call it, which is what keeps a message from reaching one channel and missing the
  other. Do not hand-roll a second recipient query.
- **Demo accounts have no chat.** `ChatAccess.ResolveIdentityAsync` returns null for them; they are shared
  throwaway logins, so a thread would leak between strangers trying the demo.

## Push

Chat sends a **fresh `Tag` per message** plus a stable `ThreadKey`. iOS WebKit ignores `renotify`, so
reusing one tag per thread swapped the tray entry in silence and follow-up messages never alerted. The
service worker closes earlier notifications matching `data.threadKey` before showing the new one, which
keeps the tray tidy without losing the alert. Other topics (turn, battle, letters) still want collapsing
and must leave `ThreadKey` null.

Deep link is `/?chat={conversationId}` — nothing character- or campaign-shaped to validate, so the link
cannot land on a thread the reader cannot open.

## UI state

`ChatState` takes identity from the circuit's claims (`ClaimTypes.NameIdentifier`), **not** from
`UserInfo.SelectedCharacter` and not from `ProtectedSessionStorage` (unreliable in `OnInitialized` on iOS
WebKit). That is why this class needs no identity-reload generations or `OnAfterRender` retries — do not
add them back. `EnsureInitializedAsync` caches the init *task*, so late callers await the first load.

`ChatMessageDTO` instances are published to every circuit, so `IsMine` must be set on a copy
(`ChatState.ForMe`). Writing it in place flips bubble alignment for other readers.

## Timestamps

Chat stores `DateTime.UtcNow`, but the Npgsql legacy timestamp switch maps every `DateTime` to
`timestamp without time zone`, so values read back have `DateTimeKind.Unspecified`. Tag before converting:
`DateTime.SpecifyKind(value, DateTimeKind.Utc).ToLocalTime()`.

## Known duplication

`ChatAccess.GameMasterUserIdsAsync` and `NotificationRecipientLookup.GameMasterUserIds` answer the same
question (Admin ∪ GameMaster, demo excluded) against different contexts — `ChatAccess` methods take the
caller's `ApplicationDbContext` so a repository operation stays on one connection. If you touch GM
resolution, change both.
